<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmagupta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 598–c. 668</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Bhillamalacharya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brahmagupta was an Indian mathematician and astronomer, born, by his own statement, in 598 CE at Bhillamāla in Gurjaradesa, modern Bhinmal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. An astronomer of the Brahmapaksha school, he composed the Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta in 628, a treatise of 24 chapters and 1008 verses covering astronomy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, geometry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a>, and later moved to Ujjaini, where he wrote the practical manual Khandakhadyaka in 665. He is credited as the first to formalise the concept of zero in mathematics and with the first clear description of the quadratic formula. He is presumed to have died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a> in 668.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brahmagupta was the son of Jishnugupta and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, in particular a Shaivite. He was born during the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavda-dynasty">Chavda dynasty</a> ruler Vyagrahamukha. Bhillamala was the capital of Gurjaradesa, the second-largest kingdom of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, which comprised southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, and it was a centre of learning for mathematics and astronomy. A later commentator, Prithudaka Svamin, called him Bhillamalacharya, the teacher from Bhillamala. He studied the five traditional Siddhantas as well as the work of astronomers including Aryabhata I, Varahamihira and Latadeva.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta is believed to be a revised version of the received Siddhanta of the Brahmapaksha school, with a considerable amount of new material. Its mathematical chapters treat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, geometry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a> and algorithmics. In chapter eighteen it gives the solution of a general linear equation and two solutions of the quadratic equation. It is the earliest known text to treat zero as a number in its own right and the first to give rules for arithmetic with zero and negative numbers. It states that zero divided by zero is zero but does not commit itself on the division of other numbers by zero.</p><p>In geometry he gave an approximate and an exact formula for the area of a cyclic quadrilateral, and a formula useful for generating Pythagorean triples. He also gave a recurrence relation for solutions to equations such as Nx² + 1 = y², now called Pell's equation, but could not apply it to every value of N; the general solution waited for Bhāskara II around 1150. For pi he used 3 as a practical value and the square root of ten as an accurate one. In astronomy he criticised rival astronomers heavily, and he argued that the Moon lies nearer the Earth than the Sun, since the Moon's phases depend on the Sun's light.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Bhāskara II, a descendant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, called him the gem of the circle of mathematicians, and commentaries on his works continued into the 12th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> came under the Arab Caliphate in 712. The court of Caliph Al-Mansur received an embassy from Sindh that brought astronomical texts, including Brahmagupta's, which Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī translated into Arabic as Sindhind. The decimal number system spread as a result. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">Al-Khwarizmi</a>'s text on Indian arithmetic was translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in the 13th century, and through such texts Brahmagupta's arithmetic algorithms spread widely. George Sarton called him one of the foremost scientists of his race and of his time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brahmagupta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
